            "creation_time": "2026-10-08T18:51:49Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "is_markdown": false,
            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19208,
            "text": "readLines() leaks its line buffer whenever an error unwinds mid-read. do_readLines() (src/main/connections.c) malloc()s the buffer it reads each line into (1000 bytes, doubled by realloc() as needed) and free()s it only on its two normal exits, so an error raised in between -- a read error or encoding-conversion failure inside Rconn_fgetc(), \"too many items\", an allocation failure for the result -- unwinds past both frees. A corrupt gzip file is enough to trigger it, so a process that reads untrusted files and catches the errors leaks 1000 bytes or more per failure.\n\nReproducible example:\n\ntf <- tempfile()\nwriteBin(as.raw(c(0x1f, 0x8b, 0x08, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 3, rep(0xaf, 40))), tf)\nreadLines(gzfile(tf, \"rt\"))\n\nObserved (R 4.6.1 and R-devel r90646): the expected \"error reading from the connection\" (after a warning about invalid compressed data), but under LeakSanitizer:\n\n==47==ERROR: LeakSanitizer: detected memory leaks\nDirect leak of 1000 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:\n    #0 malloc\n    #1 do_readLines src/main/connections.c:4472\n    #2 bcEval_loop  src/main/eval.c:8150\n\nExpected: the same error with nothing leaked.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nThe buffer is allocated at connections.c:4472 with malloc() and released by the free() calls at the two returns (lines 4520 and 4537 in r90646). The error paths between them are all longjmps out of the function: Rconn_fgetc() signals a read or conversion error, allocVector() can fail, and the \"too many items\" check raises directly. The connection itself is already protected by a cleanup context when readLines() opened it, but that context does not know about the buffer.\n\nProposed patch: attached as readlines-buffer-leak.diff. It takes the buffer from R_alloc() instead, growing it by allocating a larger block and copying, and releases it with vmaxset() on the normal exits; memory from R_alloc() is reclaimed by the error unwind, so nothing leaks whichever way the function leaves. Checked on a rebuilt R: plain files, lines longer than the initial buffer, an incomplete final line, gzip input and chunked reads from an open connection return the same results as before, and 2000 consecutive error unwinds run cleanly.\n\nFound by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (r-devel/r-oss-fuzz).\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/363, where CI results can be seen.",
            "attachment_id": {},
            "tags": [],
