            "creator": "luke",
            "creation_time": "2026-10-08T21:15:24Z",
            "count": 2,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "Thanks.\n\nOne worry about the proposed patch is that after allocating a new larger buffer with `R_alloc` the old one is not released until the end of the call. May not be a big issue but would be nice to avoid. We could add `R_realloc` or some such that addresses this.",
            "bug_id": 19208,
            "attachment_id": {},
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-10-08T21:15:24Z",
            "id": 103817
          },
          {
            "time": "2026-10-08T23:33:44Z",
            "id": 103818,
            "is_private": false,
            "attachment_id": 3901,
            "tags": [],
            "text": "Created attachment 3901\npatch v2: R_realloc() for the buffer, vmaxset() mark check, NEWS and R-exts\n\nGood idea, thanks. The updated patch adds R_realloc() and uses it for the buffer growth.\n\nR_realloc()\n\n    char *R_realloc(void *p, size_t nelem, int eltsize);\n\nSemantics follow realloc(): contents are kept up to the smaller size, a NULL p allocates, a zero size releases the block and returns NULL. The new block takes the old one's position on the R_alloc stack and the old node is unlinked, so it becomes garbage immediately while a vmaxset() to a mark taken before the original allocation still releases everything, as does an error unwind. The relink goes through CHECK_OLD_TO_NEW when the predecessor is older.\n\nStale marks\n\nAs with pointers into a realloc()ed block, a vmaxget() mark taken while p was the most recent allocation is invalidated. The R-exts text documents this and the rule (record the mark before allocating the block). To catch mistakes, vmaxset() in a PROTECTCHECK build (--enable-strict-barrier) now walks the stack and signals an error for a mark that is no longer on it, which also catches reusing a mark after a lower vmaxset() has popped it. If the unwind target's own mark is stale the state cannot be repaired, so that case calls R_Suicide() rather than recursing.\n\nreadLines()\n\n    buf_size *= 2;\n    buf = R_realloc(buf, buf_size, sizeof(char));\n\nTesting\n\nThe readLines() cases from before (long lines across several doublings, incomplete final line, gzip, chunked reads, 2000 error unwinds) behave the same, also under gctorture(TRUE). An R CMD SHLIB test exercises R_realloc() directly: growing and shrinking a block buried under up to 200 later R_alloc()s with R_gc() between steps, the same-size, NULL and zero-size cases, the error for a foreign pointer, and stack depth and vmaxset() behaviour, with and without gctorture. In a strict-barrier build a deliberately stale mark is refused and the session stays usable. make check passes in the normal build; the strict-barrier build only fails the pre-existing \"inserting non-tracking CDR in tracking cell\" assertion in reg-tests-1d.R, which fails identically on unmodified trunk.\n\nThe patch (readlines-buffer-leak-v2.diff, against r90646) also includes a NEWS entry and the R-exts text. The GitHub PR at https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/363 has been updated to match, where CI results can be seen.",
            "bug_id": 19208,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "count": 3,
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-10-08T23:33:44Z"
          },
          {
            "time": "2026-10-09T03:26:44Z",
